@article{VonHofsten1983,
abstract = {15 34–36 wk old infants were induced to reach for fast-moving objects whose velocity and starting position varied. Analysis of 144 reaches by a technique that took into consideration the 3-dimensional properties of reaches revealed that reaches in all conditions were aimed close to the meeting point with the object. The precision in timing of a reach was about .05 sec, and the systematic timing errors were close to zero. Results suggest that the infant reaches in reference to a coordinate system fixed to the moving object rather than the static background. It is concluded that the capacity to coordinate one's movements to catch a moving object is a basic and early developed skill. (13 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2012 APA, all rights reserved)},
